1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,060
Eye patches. Parrots.

2
00:00:02,700 --> 00:00:03,400
Walking the plank.

3
00:00:03,640 --> 00:00:04,360
Buried treasure.

4
00:00:04,760 --> 00:00:06,020
Everything we think we know about

5
00:00:06,020 --> 00:00:07,920
pirates... came from somewhere.

6
00:00:08,320 --> 00:00:09,480
But a lot of it came from books

7
00:00:09,480 --> 00:00:11,820
and movies, not from real pirates.

8
00:00:12,220 --> 00:00:14,620
Today we check ten famous pirate "facts".

9
00:00:15,020 --> 00:00:15,760
Some are total myths.

10
00:00:16,400 --> 00:00:17,800
And some are real...

11
00:00:17,800 --> 00:00:19,440
and much stranger than the movies.

12
00:00:22,160 --> 00:00:24,840
Number one: pirates talked like this.

13
00:00:25,480 --> 00:00:26,220
Arrr!

14
00:00:26,620 --> 00:00:28,380
Myth. Almost certainly.

15
00:00:28,780 --> 00:00:31,000
That accent comes from one actor: Robert

16
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:33,100
Newton, who played Long John Silver in a

17
00:00:33,100 --> 00:00:34,920
1950 Treasure Island film.

18
00:00:35,320 --> 00:00:36,880
He used a big version of the accent

19
00:00:36,880 --> 00:00:38,380
from his home in the West Country of

20
00:00:38,380 --> 00:00:40,600
England, where "arr" can simply mean

21
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:41,300
"yes".

22
00:00:41,460 --> 00:00:43,340
Real pirates came from all over the world:

23
00:00:43,880 --> 00:00:45,940
England, Wales, Africa, the Netherlands,

24
00:00:46,340 --> 00:00:47,320
the Caribbean...

25
00:00:47,890 --> 00:00:49,470
so there was no single pirate voice.

26
00:00:52,190 --> 00:00:55,370
Number two: every pirate had a parrot on

27
00:00:55,370 --> 00:00:56,070
his shoulder.

28
00:00:56,370 --> 00:00:57,290
Mostly a myth.

29
00:00:57,690 --> 00:00:59,250
Sailors did bring parrots back from the

30
00:00:59,250 --> 00:01:01,350
tropics. But for pirates, a parrot was

31
00:01:01,350 --> 00:01:03,190
usually cargo, something to sell.

32
00:01:03,590 --> 00:01:05,390
In the port markets of Jamaica, exotic

33
00:01:05,390 --> 00:01:07,030
birds were sold for good money.

34
00:01:07,430 --> 00:01:09,210
The famous shoulder parrot comes from a

35
00:01:09,210 --> 00:01:11,130
book: Long John Silver's parrot in

36
00:01:11,130 --> 00:01:12,870
Treasure Island, from 1883.

37
00:01:15,590 --> 00:01:18,710
Number three: pirates wore eye patches.

38
00:01:19,110 --> 00:01:21,470
Some did. But probably not for the reason

39
00:01:21,470 --> 00:01:22,170
you've heard.

40
00:01:22,550 --> 00:01:24,690
There's a popular theory: keep one eye

41
00:01:24,690 --> 00:01:26,470
covered, so it's ready for the dark below

42
00:01:26,470 --> 00:01:27,170
deck.

43
00:01:27,270 --> 00:01:29,330
Your eyes really do need many minutes to

44
00:01:29,330 --> 00:01:31,230
adjust to darkness, so the trick could

45
00:01:31,230 --> 00:01:31,930
work.

46
00:01:32,150 --> 00:01:34,170
But historians have found no letters,

47
00:01:34,430 --> 00:01:36,730
diaries or pictures of pirates actually

48
00:01:36,730 --> 00:01:37,530
doing it.

49
00:01:37,930 --> 00:01:39,490
Most pirates who wore a patch simply

50
00:01:39,490 --> 00:01:41,170
covered an eye they had lost in a

51
00:01:41,170 --> 00:01:42,250
fight or an accident.

52
00:01:44,970 --> 00:01:48,290
Number four: pirates made prisoners walk

53
00:01:48,290 --> 00:01:49,010
the plank.

54
00:01:49,410 --> 00:01:50,210
Almost never.

55
00:01:50,610 --> 00:01:52,310
Historians can prove only a handful of

56
00:01:52,310 --> 00:01:54,790
cases, and most are from long after the

57
00:01:54,790 --> 00:01:56,090
famous pirates were gone.

58
00:01:56,490 --> 00:01:57,890
The plank became famous thanks to

59
00:01:57,890 --> 00:02:00,290
storybooks, and then Treasure Island and

60
00:02:00,290 --> 00:02:01,010
Peter Pan.

61
00:02:01,410 --> 00:02:03,370
The real punishment pirates feared was

62
00:02:03,370 --> 00:02:05,830
marooning: being left alone on a tiny

63
00:02:05,830 --> 00:02:07,470
island, with almost nothing.

64
00:02:10,190 --> 00:02:12,630
Number five: pirates buried their

65
00:02:12,630 --> 00:02:14,770
treasure, and drew a map with an X.

66
00:02:15,170 --> 00:02:15,870
Myth.

67
00:02:16,090 --> 00:02:17,970
Pirates usually spent their loot fast, on

68
00:02:17,970 --> 00:02:19,710
food, drink and fun in port.

69
00:02:20,190 --> 00:02:21,730
Why hide it in the sand?

70
00:02:22,380 --> 00:02:24,760
There is one famous exception: Captain

71
00:02:24,760 --> 00:02:25,720
William Kidd.

72
00:02:26,120 --> 00:02:29,060
In 1699, knowing he was hunted, he buried

73
00:02:29,060 --> 00:02:31,040
some treasure on Gardiners Island, near

74
00:02:31,040 --> 00:02:31,740
New York.

75
00:02:32,040 --> 00:02:33,720
It was dug up the same year, and

76
00:02:33,720 --> 00:02:35,440
used as evidence against him.

77
00:02:35,840 --> 00:02:37,220
And treasure maps with an X?

78
00:02:37,420 --> 00:02:39,240
They come from stories, most of all

79
00:02:39,240 --> 00:02:40,220
Treasure Island.

80
00:02:43,020 --> 00:02:45,100
Number six: pirates flew the skull and

81
00:02:45,100 --> 00:02:45,800
crossbones.

82
00:02:46,180 --> 00:02:48,660
True! But the reason will surprise you.

83
00:02:49,060 --> 00:02:50,240
The flag wasn't for show.

84
00:02:50,520 --> 00:02:52,780
It was a warning: surrender now, and

85
00:02:52,780 --> 00:02:53,720
nobody gets hurt.

86
00:02:54,120 --> 00:02:55,940
A real fight could sink the ship pirates

87
00:02:55,940 --> 00:02:58,040
wanted to steal, and kill their own crew.

88
00:02:58,440 --> 00:03:00,580
So the scarier the flag, the more often

89
00:03:00,580 --> 00:03:01,920
ships simply gave up.

90
00:03:02,320 --> 00:03:04,700
And if a ship refused, some pirates raised

91
00:03:04,700 --> 00:03:05,940
a red flag instead.

92
00:03:06,500 --> 00:03:07,900
That meant: no mercy.

93
00:03:10,620 --> 00:03:13,340
Number seven: pirates had no rules.

94
00:03:13,740 --> 00:03:16,540
Myth. Pirates signed written rules called

95
00:03:16,540 --> 00:03:17,240
articles.

96
00:03:17,560 --> 00:03:19,500
One famous pirate, Bartholomew Roberts,

97
00:03:19,660 --> 00:03:21,940
had a crew rule: lights and candles out

98
00:03:21,940 --> 00:03:23,320
at eight o'clock at night.

99
00:03:23,720 --> 00:03:24,820
Want to keep drinking after that?

100
00:03:25,280 --> 00:03:27,060
Go and sit on the open deck, in

101
00:03:27,060 --> 00:03:27,760
the dark.

102
00:03:27,920 --> 00:03:29,520
His crew couldn't gamble for money on

103
00:03:29,520 --> 00:03:31,080
board, and they couldn't fight each other

104
00:03:31,080 --> 00:03:31,780
on the ship.

105
00:03:32,160 --> 00:03:34,100
Arguments were settled on land instead.

106
00:03:36,900 --> 00:03:38,760
Number eight: pirates got injury

107
00:03:38,760 --> 00:03:39,480
insurance.

108
00:03:39,880 --> 00:03:41,220
Believe it or not...

109
00:03:41,220 --> 00:03:41,840
true.

110
00:03:41,840 --> 00:03:44,720
One buccaneer crew in the 1670s agreed on

111
00:03:44,720 --> 00:03:47,100
a price list for injuries, paid from the

112
00:03:47,100 --> 00:03:47,800
shared loot.

113
00:03:48,120 --> 00:03:48,820
Lose your right arm?

114
00:03:49,420 --> 00:03:50,520
Six hundred pieces of eight.

115
00:03:50,960 --> 00:03:52,720
Your left arm, five hundred.

116
00:03:53,120 --> 00:03:54,720
A right leg, five hundred.

117
00:03:55,280 --> 00:03:57,580
An eye or a finger, one hundred.

118
00:03:57,980 --> 00:03:59,980
A small fortune for an ordinary sailor,

119
00:04:00,300 --> 00:04:02,220
agreed before the voyage even began.

120
00:04:04,940 --> 00:04:08,180
Number nine: the captain was the boss.

121
00:04:08,580 --> 00:04:09,700
Only sometimes.

122
00:04:10,100 --> 00:04:12,280
On many pirate ships, the crew voted for

123
00:04:12,280 --> 00:04:14,360
their captain... and could vote him out.

124
00:04:14,760 --> 00:04:16,180
In battle, his word was law.

125
00:04:16,460 --> 00:04:18,340
But the rest of the time, another elected

126
00:04:18,340 --> 00:04:20,680
officer, the quartermaster, handled food,

127
00:04:21,160 --> 00:04:22,060
loot and fights.

128
00:04:22,460 --> 00:04:24,240
On a navy ship, you obeyed or you

129
00:04:24,240 --> 00:04:26,920
were punished. On a pirate ship, you got

130
00:04:26,920 --> 00:04:27,400
a vote.

131
00:04:30,200 --> 00:04:32,560
And number ten: some of the most famous

132
00:04:32,560 --> 00:04:34,120
pirates were women.

133
00:04:34,520 --> 00:04:35,160
True.

134
00:04:35,160 --> 00:04:38,000
In 1720, Anne Bonny and Mary Read sailed

135
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:39,960
with the pirate Calico Jack in the

136
00:04:39,960 --> 00:04:40,660
Caribbean.

137
00:04:40,880 --> 00:04:42,380
When their ship was captured, the two

138
00:04:42,380 --> 00:04:44,420
women were put on trial in Jamaica...

139
00:04:44,420 --> 00:04:45,960
and both were sentenced to hang.

140
00:04:46,360 --> 00:04:47,600
But they told the court they were

141
00:04:47,600 --> 00:04:49,840
expecting babies, so their executions were

142
00:04:49,840 --> 00:04:50,540
stopped.

143
00:04:50,740 --> 00:04:52,380
Mary Read died in prison the next year.

144
00:04:53,220 --> 00:04:53,840
And Anne Bonny?

145
00:04:54,920 --> 00:04:56,400
Nobody knows for sure what happened to

146
00:04:56,400 --> 00:04:56,980
her.

147
00:04:56,980 --> 00:05:01,780
So: no "arrr", few parrots, almost no

148
00:05:01,780 --> 00:05:04,320
planks, and hardly any buried treasure.

149
00:05:04,720 --> 00:05:07,120
But written rules, injury insurance,

150
00:05:07,560 --> 00:05:09,400
elections, and women pirates?

151
00:05:10,080 --> 00:05:10,780
All real.

152
00:05:10,980 --> 00:05:12,660
The real pirates were stranger than the

153
00:05:12,660 --> 00:05:13,820
movies ever showed.

154
00:05:14,220 --> 00:05:15,380
Which one surprised you the most?

155
00:05:15,920 --> 00:05:16,700
Tell us in the comments.
